{=============================================================================
   Domestic hot-water storage tank (500 L): dynamic temperature evolution
   CoolSolve Library model CSL-0009
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Transient energy balance of a fully mixed (non-stratified) 500 L domestic
   hot-water tank, initially at 60 C, over a 5-hour scenario:
     - 0-1 h   : 10 kW electric heater on,
     - 1-2 h   : heater off,
     - 2-4 h   : constant tap draw of 0.05 kg/s, replaced by city water at 12 C,
     - 4-5 h   : no draw,
   with continuous ambient losses (AU_amb = 20 W/K, T_amb = 20 C).

   The tank temperature T_tank is the (implicit) state variable: the energy
   change DELTAu since t = 0 is both the time integral of dU/dtau and
   M_tank*cp_tank*(T_tank - T_tank_0).

   This file keeps the native EES syntax of the original exercise (5-argument
   IF(A,B,X,Y,Z), symbolic integration limits tau_1, tau_2, ';'-separated
   $IntegralTable). Status: verified; solve time about 1.5 min, see README.md.

   Inputs : T_tank_0, T_in_tank, T_amb, P_atm, cp_w, cp_tank, Vol_tank, AU_amb,
            heater and draw schedules
   Outputs: T_tank(tau) trajectory, dU/dtau, Q_dot_tap, Q_dot_amb, Q_dot_res
   Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
   Source : ULiège course MECA0002-1 "Thermodynamique appliquée et introduction
            aux machines thermiques", repetition 3, exercise 3 - see README.md
 =============================================================================}

"Temperatures"
T_tank_0 = 60 [C]     "initial tank temperature [C]"
T_in_tank = 12 [C]    "city (make-up) water temperature [C]"
T_amb = 20 [C]        "ambient temperature [C]"

"Atmospheric pressure (about 1 bar) and constant water cp"
P_atm = 100000 [Pa]           "atmospheric pressure [Pa]"
cp_w = 4186 [J/(kg·C)]        "specific heat of the water [J/kg-K]"
cp_tank = 4186 [J/(kg·C)]     "specific heat of the tank content [J/kg-K]"

"Tank geometry and mass"
Vol_tank = 0.5 [m^3]                        "tank volume [m^3]"
v_tank = volume(Water, T=T_tank_0, P=P_atm) "specific volume of water at the initial state [m^3/kg]"
M_tank = Vol_tank/v_tank                    "mass of water in the tank [kg]"

{Transient first law (kinetic and potential energy neglected):
 Q + W - m_dot*Deltah = d(mu)/dt = dU/dt
 What we need is dU/dtau, to follow the internal energy (i.e. the temperature)
 of the tank in time:
   Q  -> ambient losses + heater power  = Q_dot_amb (negative) + Q_dot_res (positive)
   W  = 0 (no work)
   -m_dot*Deltah -> enthalpy flow of the make-up water = Q_dot_tap (negative;
                     can be seen as a thermal loss of the system)}

"Heater schedule: 10 kW between tau = 0 and 3600 s, then off"
Q_dot_res = if(tau, 3600[s], 10000[W], 10000[W], 0[W])
{EES help (fchart.com/ees/eeshelp/_if.htm): IF(A,B,X,Y,Z) returns X if A<B,
 Y if A=B and Z if A>B.}

"Tap-draw schedule"
{The draw lasts 2 h and starts after 2 h, and the simulation covers 5 h, so
 between the 4th and 5th hour there is no draw any more. Two IF functions
 (and the intermediate variable M_dot_in_tank1) are used: the first one marks
 the start of the draw after 7200 s, the second one its end at 14400 s.}
M_dot_in_tank1 = if(tau, 7200[s], 0[kg/s], 0.05[kg/s], 0.05[kg/s])  "draw rate after 7200 s [kg/s]"
M_dot_in_tank = if(tau, 14400[s], M_dot_in_tank1, M_dot_in_tank1, 0[kg/s])  "draw rate [kg/s]"

"Draw power"
Q_dot_tap = -M_dot_in_tank*cp_w*(T_tank-T_in_tank)
{No stratification: the drawn water leaves at T_out_tank = T_tank. T_tank is
 not known at this stage: it is precisely the unknown found by the integration.
 Equivalently, through enthalpies:
   Q_dot_tap = -M_dot_in_tank*(h_tap_out - h_tap_in)
   h_out = enthalpy(water, p=p_atm, T=T_tank)
   h_in  = enthalpy(water, p=p_atm, T=T_in_tank)
 (negative because lost by the system; T_tank > T_in_tank)}

"Ambient losses of the tank"
AU_amb = 20 [W/C]                     "global heat-exchange coefficient tank-ambient [W/K]"
Q_dot_amb = -AU_amb*(T_tank-T_amb)    "(negative because lost by the system; T_tank > T_amb)"

"Tank balance: transient first law Q + W - m_dot*Deltah = dU/dt"
dU\dtau = Q_dot_res + Q_dot_tap + Q_dot_amb    "rate of change of the tank internal energy [W]"

"Integration of the tank power over time to follow its temperature"
DELTAu = integral(dU\dtau, tau, tau_1, tau_2, 1)   "energy change since tau = 0 [J]; last term = integration step (1 s)"
DELTAu = M_tank*cp_tank*(T_tank-T_tank_0)          "same energy change, through the tank temperature [J]"
tau_1 = 0 [s]          "start of the integration [s]"
tau_2 = 5*3600 [s]     "end of the integration: 5 hours simulated [s]"

$IntegralTable tau:1; dU\dtau; Q_dot_tap; Q_dot_amb; Q_dot_res;  M_dot_in_tank; t_tank
{Creates an Integral Table (Ctrl+I) with all the variables of interest, to
 plot the trajectories (New Plot = Ctrl+Alt+X). EES accepts ; or , as column
 separators.}
